// Machine prototypes (work item 0011): entities placed in the world by an
// item, loaded after items.sjson and resolved to a dense Machine_Id.
// item is the item that places the machine; picking the machine up gives
// it back. Each item places at most one machine.
// name_key is a key into data/strings/en.sjson.
// description_key (optional, work item 0070) is the key of the text the
// machine panel shows under the name: describe_machine_<id> by
// convention. A set key must exist.
// footprint is width (x), depth (z) and height (y) in blocks before
// rotation, each from 1 to 12; a quarter turn swaps width and depth.
// model (optional, work item 0055) names data/models/<model>.obj with its
// .mtl when that file exists (work item 0204), else data/models/
// <model>.vox, drawn in place of the coloured box and turned with the
// machine. Its +x side is the front, the side the rotation points at. A
// named file must exist and load. An OBJ is in cells of the unrotated
// footprint (x and z centred, y from the bottom, +x the front, y up), is
// not scaled, must stay within the footprint's x and z (0.02 cells of
// slack) and above its bottom, and may rise above its height. Its group
// part is the moving part (the motion paragraph's <model>_part.vox for a
// voxel model). A material with a non-zero Ke is emissive. A .vox is a
// MagicaVoxel model scaled to the footprint (8 or 16 voxels per block),
// and its palette indices 240 to 255 are emissive (work item 0056).
// Emissive faces ignore the world light and light up while the machine
// works. tools/make_models.py writes the OBJ models (in Blender) and
// tools/make_placeholder_models.py the voxel ones.
// motion (optional, work item 0056, needs a model) moves one part of the
// model while the machine works and rests it otherwise: {kind, axis,
// amplitude, period_seconds, pivot}. kind pump moves the part along
// axis (x, y or z of the unrotated footprint) by amplitude blocks and back,
// bob up to amplitude blocks either way, spin turns it about the axis
// through pivot by amplitude turns per period, swing turns it by amplitude
// turns and back, and glow moves nothing but pulses the emissive voxels.
// Every kind but glow needs data/models/<model>_part.vox, the part alone at
// the body's size and frame. pivot is [x, y, z] in blocks from the
// footprint's minimum corner. kind arm (inserters only, work item 0175)
// names the arm's part files instead, <model>.vox for the base and
// <model>_turret.vox and the rest (doc/presentation.md, Machine models),
// posed from the inserter's cycle, not the clock, so it takes no other
// field. kind slide (hatches only, work item 0198) moves the part along
// axis by amplitude blocks (0 to 12) times the hatch's open fraction,
// eased over period_seconds from its last toggle, and needs the part
// group of its OBJ.
// kind selects the panel and the simulation: chest, furnace, capsule,
// belt, inserter, drill, splitter, pipe, offshore_pump, boiler,
// steam_engine, storage_tank, pump, tar_pit_pump, flare_stack,
// combustion_generator, hydro_turbine, pole, power_switch, lamp,
// crafting_machine, lab, schematic_crate, core_sample_drill,
// launch_pad, foundation, belt_pole, pod, crafting_station, tree, hatch,
// locker, crafting_bench or oxygen_generator.
// tree (work item 0197, doc/content.md, Trees) is a tree's model:
// placed by the planet's generation, never by an item, never an entity;
// only footprint and model are read.
// former_ids (optional, work item 0196) lists ids this record had in an
// older build: a save naming one loads it as this record, with one log
// line. A former id is never a current id of the table, nor listed twice.
// The pod (work items 0179, 0199, 0198, doc/content.md, The pod) is the
// start on the field: the session stands it on the floor of the home's
// crater on a free frame of its own, no pad under it. It has no item,
// cannot be picked up, and has no panel and no slots; its footprint is in
// cells of the foundation pitch. fixtures (pods only, at most 8) lists
// {machine, cell = {x, y, z}, rotation}: a machine of kind hatch, locker,
// crafting_bench or oxygen_generator placed by the world with its minimum
// corner at cell of the pod's unrotated footprint and turned by rotation
// quarter turns against the pod. Its box (the rotated footprint from
// cell) lies inside the pod's footprint, overlaps no other fixture's box
// and neither the cabin's spawn cells nor an open_cells box; the pod
// leaves those cells to it. Hatches (kind hatch, work item 0198): no
// item, at least 2 cells high, no open_cells, a slide or a spin motion
// (a spin of at most 1 turn, 0221) or none. Closed its cells are solid; open
// none is, and every row but the top is an open cell. Lockers (kind
// locker): a chest placed by the world, slots 8 to 48. Crafting benches
// (kind crafting_bench): recipe_maker hand, no slots, power, speed or
// ports; the panel is the crafting station's. Oxygen generators (kind
// oxygen_generator): no slots, power or ports; a sealed room one touches
// has an unlimited supply.
// open_cells (optional, work item 0186, at most 4) lists boxes of
// footprint cells the player walks through: {from = {x, y, z}, to = {x,
// y, z}}, both inclusive, in cells of the unrotated footprint (x along the
// width, y up, z along the depth). The cells are still the machine's, so
// nothing is placed on them, but they are not solid. The pod opens the
// floor before its chair, the lane to the airlock and the airlock's bore;
// the player stands on the crater's floor under them.
// lights (optional, work item 0224, at most 8) lists {position = [x, y,
// z], color = [r, g, b], radius_cells}: a lamp at position in cells of
// the model's frame (x and z centred on the unrotated footprint, y from
// its bottom, +x the front: the OBJ's numbers), inside the footprint with
// 0.02 cells of slack, color 0 to 255 per channel, radius_cells 1 to 64.
// A lamp may add clip = false (work item 0229): by default a lamp lights
// only inside its machine's box (the footprint, up to the model's top
// when it rises higher) and gives nothing to a face turned away from it,
// so a lamp inside a hull leaves the hull's outside dark; with clip =
// false it shines out of the machine (a street lamp).
// Each shines a point light in a field session while the machine's model
// works (the same flag that lights its emissive materials; a pod always
// works); the block world takes no point lights. Presentation only. A
// machine whose model draw_entities does not draw (fluid and power
// machines, inserters, trees) may list lamps, which then do nothing.
// A machine whose model has data/models/<model>.collision.sjson (work item
// 0230, doc/content.md, Models) collides by its volumes on a frame: its
// cells stay its own for placement, its open cells for the spawn, the
// fixtures and the sealed room, but the player and the aiming ray meet the
// volumes.
// interior_light_share (optional, pods only, work item 0225) is 0 to 1:
// the pod's model, its fixtures' models and the bodies of players
// standing in its footprint are lit at this share of the light round
// them, never below the models' floor, before the point lights add, so
// the cabin is dark but where its lamps reach; the emissive materials of
// a working model keep their brightness. 1 when absent. Presentation
// only.
// The capsule (work item 0013) stands on the landing pad from the start:
// it has no item, cannot be picked up, and holds 8 slots for quest
// rewards and deliveries.
// Schematic crates (kind schematic_crate, work item 0036) stand in cave
// pockets where world generation puts them: 1 by 1 by 1, no item, never
// picked up, one slot holding a schematic that Interact takes and reads.
// Chests: slots is the number of item slots.
// Furnaces: fuel_slots and input_slots must be 1 and output_slots 2: the
// main output and the byproduct (slag) slot of work item 0027. speed
// multiplies the recipe rate (1 is the listed recipe time).
// fuel_power_kilowatts is the power drawn from the fuel while working.
// Belts (work item 0014): belt_shape is flat, ramp or lift (the item
// placed decides the shape; a ramp's up or down and a lift's direction
// are chosen at placement). belt_speed_blocks_per_second is the item speed
// along the belt; it becomes a whole number of 1/256 block units per tick,
// so pick values where speed * 256 / tick rate is an integer (1.875 at
// 60 ticks per second is 8 units). Belt 2 (work item 0037) runs at 3.75,
// 16 units per tick: a line ends where the speed changes, and a drag or
// an automatic ramp uses the ramp of the same speed as the belt placed.
// Inserters (work item 0015): items_per_minute sets the cycle, which is
// 60 * tick rate / items_per_minute ticks (100 at 36 per minute), half a
// swing to the drop side and half back. A burner inserter has fuel_slots
// = 1 and fuel_power_kilowatts, burned only while the arm moves. Electric
// inserters have electric_power_kilowatts, drawn only while the arm
// moves (work item 0020). filter_slots = 1 makes a filter inserter.
// inserter_reach (optional, work item 0037, default 1) is how many cells
// behind the inserter it picks from and how many ahead it drops into on
// the block world's frame: 2 for the long inserter. reach_millimetres
// (work item 0175, 250 to 8000) is the arm's reach unfolded: on a
// foundation frame it reaches that over the frame's pitch in whole cells
// (2000 at 500 mm is four). The block frame keeps inserter_reach until
// the block world is retired (M14).
// Drills (work item 0016): placed with at least one footprint cell on a
// vein outcrop, they draw one unit per cycle from the vein's reservoir,
// picked by the vein type's output mix, and drop it into the cell in
// front of their arrow. items_per_minute is the ore rate on a vein whose
// ore share is rate_reference_ore_percent (the iron vein of
// veins.sjson, 80 percent hematite), so the cycle is
// 60 * tick rate * rate_reference_ore_percent / (100 * items_per_minute)
// ticks (192 at 15 per minute and 80 percent), and the spoil comes on top.
// The footprint must be square, so turning a placed drill moves no cell.
// fuel_power_kilowatts is burned only on the ticks the drill works.
// Bore drills (work item 0035): a drill with boring_seconds taps a deep
// vein instead of an outcrop. It is placed on solid ground with the centre
// column of its footprint inside a deep vein's disc, then bores for
// boring_seconds (ticks of work, so a brownout lengthens it) before its
// first cycle. The rate rule is the surface drill's.
// revival_port = true (work item 0035) gives a drill one input fluid port
// with a fluid filter (the mining fluid): once its finite vein is
// exhausted, the drill keeps producing at half rate while the port holds
// enough, taking 10 litres per unit, drawing by the vein type's mix as if
// the reservoir were full. It has no effect on infinite veins.
// Splitters (work item 0017): stand across two belt cells, so the
// footprint is 1 along the flow (width) and 2 across it (depth);
// belt_speed_blocks_per_second is the speed of items inside it, like the
// belt it joins.
// Fluids (work item 0019, doc/fluids.md): fluid_ports lists a machine's
// ports, each with its own buffer: cell {x, y, z} of the unrotated
// footprint (x along the width, y up, z along the depth), face
// (positive_x, negative_x, positive_y, negative_y, positive_z, negative_z,
// pointing out of the footprint, or "all" for every outward face of the
// footprint), direction (input takes from the network, output gives to
// it, both does either), fluid (optional, a fluids.sjson id the port only
// ever holds), phase (optional, liquid or gas: the only phase the port
// admits, work item 0030, or burnable_gas: gases with a
// fuel_kilojoules_per_litre only, work item 0033) and buffer_litres. Ports turn with the
// footprint.
// Pipes: buffer_litres per block and flow_litres_per_second, the most a
// connection between two segments moves (1200 is 20 litres per tick).
// Offshore pump: fluid_litres_per_second into its one output port while
// there is room and it has electric_power_kilowatts (work item 0140),
// scaled by its network's satisfaction like the pump. Placed with the
// cell in front of its +x end (its intake, the end without the port), at
// its height or one below, a water source.
// Boiler: turns fluid_litres_per_second of its input port's fluid into as
// much of its output port's fluid, burning fuel_power_kilowatts from its
// fuel slot only while it does.
// Steam engine: input ports only. A generator (work item 0020): gives up
// to electric_output_kilowatts to its power network, burning
// fluid_litres_per_second of steam at full output, so a litre of steam is
// electric_output_kilowatts * 1000 / fluid_litres_per_second joules
// (30 kJ), drawn only for the energy delivered.
// Storage tank: one port on every face, its buffer is the tank.
// Pump: moves fluid_litres_per_second from its input to its output port
// whatever the heights, and only with electric_power_kilowatts.
// Tar pit pump (work item 0030): placed like the offshore pump, with a
// block whose fluid_source is its port's fluid (a tar pit) in front of its
// intake. Pumps fluid_litres_per_minute into its one output port while it
// has electric_power_kilowatts, whole litres as the minute adds up. 600 L
// per minute (work item 0031) lets two pumps feed one refinery.
// Flare stack (work item 0030): one input port with phase = "gas"; while
// powered it destroys up to fluid_litres_per_second of the gas in it,
// counted as voided in the statistics. The power is the price of the sink.
// Since work item 0033 it is a relief valve: it burns only while its port
// is at least 90 percent full, so consumers on the same gas network are
// served first.
// Combustion generator (work item 0032): one input port with phase =
// "burnable_gas" (work item 0033, so steam stays out) and fuel_slots = 1.
// A generator like the steam engine: gives up to
// electric_output_kilowatts to its power network, burning the gas in its
// port first, whole litres of fuel_kilojoules_per_litre (fluids.sjson),
// then solid fuel from its slot, only for the energy delivered.
// fuel_efficiency_percent (work item 0140, required on this kind only, 1
// to 100) is the share of a fuel item's energy it turns into electricity.
// Without fluid ports it is a fuel generator, burning items only.
// Hydro turbine (work item 0037): a generator without fuel or ports. Placed
// in air or water with at least one footprint cell holding flowing water
// of hydro_minimum_water_level or more (source water does not count), and
// water keeps flowing through its cells. It offers
// hydro_kilowatts_per_water_level times the sum of the flowing water
// levels in its footprint, up to electric_output_kilowatts, read from the
// blocks every tick.
// Power (work item 0020, doc/fluids.md): electric machines (any with
// electric_power_kilowatts: electric inserters, electric drills, pumps,
// lamps) and generators (steam engines, combustion generators) belong to
// the network of a pole whose supply_volume covers one of their cells. A
// drill without a fuel slot and with electric_power_kilowatts is an
// electric drill.
// dispatch_order (work item 0140, required on every generator kind and
// refused elsewhere, 0 to 9): generators with the lowest order serve
// their network first, up to their offers; the next order takes only
// what is left, so the fuel generator (3) burns nothing while the steam
// engines (1) cover the load. Within one order the delivered energy is
// split in proportion to the offers. Hydro turbine 0, combustion
// generator 2.
// Poles: supply_volume is width (x), depth (z) and height (y) of the box
// of cells powered, centred across on the pole's square footprint (so the
// volume's width and depth differ from the footprint's by an even count,
// work item 0037) and starting at the pole's bottom. wire_reach connects it to every pole or
// power switch whose footprint centre (across, at the bottom) is at most
// that far from its own (straight line, the shorter reach of the two
// counts; work item 0038 moved this from the origin corner).
// Power switch: a 1 by 1 by 1 pole without a supply volume that joins the
// networks it wires while on and splits them while off.
// Lamp: electric_power_kilowatts and light_level, the block light it
// gives while its network is satisfied above half, and optionally
// light_color as for blocks (blocks.sjson), white when left out.
// Crafting machines (work items 0021 and 0026): speed, recipe_maker (the
// made_in category of recipes.sjson it makes: assembler, crusher, washer,
// alloy_furnace, refinery, cracking, chemistry, gasifier, electrolysis or
// stone_cutting) and either
// electric_power_kilowatts or fuel_slots = 1 with fuel_power_kilowatts.
// recipe_choice chosen: the player picks the
// recipe in the panel, with one input slot per ingredient and one output
// slot per product of that recipe, and no slot fields here. recipe_choice
// fixed: input_slots and output_slots are set here, and the machine makes
// the recipe of its category whose input items are exactly the loaded
// ones (the loader refuses a category where one recipe's input items are
// a subset of another's). Optional input fluid_ports feed recipes with
// fluid_inputs; optional output fluid_ports take fluid_outputs, the first
// fluid output into the first output port in port order and so on (work
// item 0030). A fixed machine with fluid ports may have no item slots:
// its recipes are then told apart by the fluids in its input ports.
// Recipes of the category must fit the slots and ports.
// Refinery (work item 0030): ports in port order crude oil in on the
// -z face, then petroleum gas out on -x, light oil out on +z and heavy oil
// out on +x (faces of the unrotated footprint, all on the bottom layer),
// each fixed to its fluid, so each fraction always leaves by the same face.
// Cracking unit: oil in on -z (heavy or light oil, whichever it holds
// picks the recipe), water in on -x, product out on +z.
// Chemical plant (work item 0031): gas in on -z (phase gas: petroleum gas
// or wood gas), a second input on -x without a filter (water, heavy oil or
// wood gas), so the fluids present together with the loaded item (coal,
// charcoal or none) pick plastic bar, sulfur, bitumen or syngas plastic.
// One item output slot shared by every product. The output port on +z
// (work item 0035) takes the mining fluid made from sulfur and water, and
// since work item 0040 rocket fuel too, so it has no fluid filter.
// Wood gasifier (work item 0031): logs in, charcoal out, wood gas out on
// +z; electric, no fuel slot.
// The recycler (work item 0027) is recipe_maker recycler, fixed: no
// recipe is made in it. It takes any item a recipe makes that items.sjson
// does not flag cannot_recycle, reverses the first recipe making it (one
// craft takes that recipe's count of the item and gives back 25 percent
// of each input count rounded down, at the recipe's time), and puts the
// returns into any output slot holding their item or an empty one. Every
// recyclable item's returns must fit the output slots.
// Electrolyser (work item 0035): fixed, one input and one output slot,
// alumina to aluminium plate and quartz to silicon.
// Core sample drill (work item 0038, kind core_sample_drill): 1 by 1 by
// 2, electric_power_kilowatts, no slots. After sampling_seconds of
// powered work it records the strata below its column and the deep vein
// under it, once; picked up and placed again it samples anew.
// Launch pad (work item 0040, kind launch_pad): electric_power_kilowatts
// drawn while assembling, slots is the cargo section (8, any item),
// launch_parts the part items with the count a rocket takes (one slot
// each, at most 3, in slot order), launch_fuel_litres of the fluid its one
// input port is filtered to, assembly_seconds of powered work (the parts
// and the fuel are taken in ten equal stages over it) and
// launch_seconds of the ascent.
// Lab (work item 0021): speed and electric_power_kilowatts, no slot
// fields. A lab has one slot per science pack item any technology in
// technologies.sjson consumes.
// Foundation (work item 0174, kind foundation): a footprint one cell
// high and color = [r, g, b] (work item 0196, required, each 0 to 255 and
// not all zero), the flat colour its slab is drawn in. Its cells are the
// solid floor of a frame that machines stand on (doc/content.md,
// Foundations); placed free it starts a frame at
// foundation_pitch_millimetres (game.sjson). The tiers are listed wooden,
// stone brick, iron: the first one is the planet preview's.
// Crafting station (work item 0196, kind crafting_station): a place the
// player crafts at by hand. recipe_maker names the made_in category its
// panel offers (not hand, furnace or recycler); its crafts run in the
// player's hand queue. No slots, fuel, power, speed or fluid ports; an
// item is required.
// Machines on bare ground (work item 0201, doc/content.md, Machines on
// bare ground): stands_on_ground (optional, default false) marks a
// machine that stands on the ground without a foundation for good: it is
// never refused for slope and never wears. Set on the belts, the pipe,
// the electric poles, the belt pole, the pod and the stone cutting table
// (a crafting station never operates, so it never wears either way).
// Every other machine placed on bare ground breaks down after
// bare_ground_life_minutes of operation (game.sjson) unless it stands
// on foundations; bare_ground_life_minutes (optional, 1 to 10080)
// overrides that for the machine.
// Belt pole (work item 0176, kind belt_pole): a 1 by 1 by 1 footprint and
// height_millimetres (250 to 4000), how high over its bottom a belt or
// pipe run ends on it (doc/content.md, Belt poles and runs). Placed free
// it is a one cell frame of its own facing the run; snapped it stands on
// a frame's foundation.
machines = [
	{
		id = "wooden_chest"
